  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 355 PM Sunday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Much quieter weather late this afternoon and \nevening with a brief shower possible. \n\nThe tornado watch has been cancelled for all of central NC and the \ndeep convection has exited the area as mid and upper layer \ndrying is spreading across the area. A moist cyclonic flow \ncontinues with some weak residual instability across the eastern\nPiedmont, Sandhills and VA border counties. A few showers may \ndevelop in this environment over the next few hours but the \ncoverage should be limited. All of the showers should wind down \nby around sunset with fair weather expected overnight. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... No significant precipitation is expected for much \nof the upcoming week. \n\nA northwest to westerly flow aloft will dominate the weather pattern \nthrough the work week supporting a generally fair and dry weather \npattern. A back door cold front tries to drop into the region \non Thursday but ensembles suggest the strong upper level ridge \nwill likely restrain the front across WV and VA. Precipitation \nwith this feature should be limited and likely north of the \narea. A stronger cold front will sweep southeast across the \nregion on Friday and Friday night, this system will be moisture \nstarved and little precipitation is expected. The generally dry \nwest and northwesterly flow will unfortunately keep the area \nprecipitation free. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... A big warmup is expected for mid week before a \nsignificant cool down by the weekend with frost possible. \n\nThe large ridge developing across the Deep South will allow morning \nlow level thickness values to range from 1400 to 1410m on Wednesday \nmorning. This will support afternoon highs in the mid to possibly \nupper 80s which are about 12 to 17 degrees above average. \n\nA significant cool down is expected this weekend with morning low \nlevel thickness values by Sunday morning ranging in the 1310 to \n1320m range. It's worth noting that the operation GFS is \nshowing its cold bias with its forecasting low level \nthicknesses on Sunday morning from 1295 to 1305m with the GFS \nMOS providing lows of 33/35 at Roxboro and Raleigh! Not ready to\njump on that degree of cold but a consensus approach supports \nlows into the upper and possibly mid 30s which would support a \nfrost risk.",
